  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nRiver valley fog expected to develop yet again tonight, but any\noverland fog will be shallow/limited.\n\nThe focus then shifts to the next system impacting the Ohio Vally\nlate Wednesday into Thursday. ILN will be placed in the warm sector\non Wednesday with a low pressure system that will be tracking along\nthe northern Great Lakes region. We will observe a bit of an increase\nin temperature and humidity levels on Wednesday compared to today. \n\nThe biggest uncertainty for any severe potential will be based on\ntiming. Some CAMs try to usher in convection into portions of \nMercer/Auglaize Counties around 7-8 PM, which is just prior to an \ninversion developing at the surface. With modest CAPE values near \n1500-2000 J/kg and Effective Shear of 30-35 kts during that period, \nsome of these storms could be strong to severe. Primary threat would \nbe damaging straight-line winds, with large hail being a secondary \nthreat. This severe threat will likely only linger for a few of our \nnorthwestern counties (primarily in the Miami Valley, perhaps some of\nSE IN) before storms weaken as they progress southeast overnight. \nShowers/storms are expected to become more scattered to isolated \nthrough the remainder of the night. \n\nDepending on the progression of the cold front, some showers/storms\nwill remain possible for areas along and SE of the I-71 corridor on\nThursday. However, the strongest storms are likely to remain east of\nour CWA.",
